[2026-03-16T01:40:34.035Z] {"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Kj7DVG6kDeMhnXmNEpHwNh","type":"tool_result","content":"    80→`RECORD_REPLAY_API_KEY` must be set in the environment. It is already available in this\n    81→container. The Playwright config passes it to the reporter via:\n    82→\n    83→```ts\n    84→apiKey: process.env.REPLAY_API_KEY ?? process.env.RECORD_REPLAY_API_KEY\n    85→```\n    86→\n    87→### 4. Playwright config\n    88→\n    89→The Playwright config MUST:\n    90→\n    91→1. Import BOTH `replayReporter` and `devices as replayDevices` from `@replayio/playwright`.\n    92→2. Include `replayReporter(...)` in the `reporter` array with `upload: false`. Uploads are\n    93→   handled by the test script, NOT by the reporter.\n    94→3. Spread `replayDevices['Replay Chromium']` into the global `use` config. This sets\n    95→   `executablePath`, `RECORD_ALL_CONTENT`, and critically `RECORD_REPLAY_METADATA_FILE` which\n    96→   is required for the reporter to attach test metadata (pass/fail result) to recordings.\n    97→   Without `RECORD_REPLAY_METADATA_FILE`, the recording driver cannot read the per-test\n    98→   metadata written by the fixture, and all recordings will have empty `testResult`.\n    99→\n   100→**IMPORTANT**: Do NOT manually set `launchOptions.executablePath` and `env` instead of using\n   101→`replayDevices['Replay Chromium']`. Manual config misses `RECORD_REPLAY_METADATA_FILE`,\n   102→which breaks the recording-to-test metadata association.\n   103→\n   104→**IMPORTANT**: The Replay Chromium browser adds instrumentation overhead that makes all\n   105→operations ~2–3x slower than standard Chromium. Always set higher timeouts from the start:\n   106→\n   107→- `actionTimeout: 15000` (15s for clicks, fills, etc.)\n   108→- `navigationTimeout: 30000` (30s for page navigations)\n   109→- Use `expect.toBeVisible({ timeout: 10000 })` for post-navigation assertions\n   110→\n   111→Example:\n   112→\n   113→```ts\n   114→import { defineConfig, devices } from '@playwright/test';\n   115→import { devices as replayDevices, replayReporter } from '@replayio/playwright';\n   116→\n   117→export default defineConfig({\n   118→  // Replay Chromium is slower due to instrumentation — use generous timeouts\n   119→  actionTimeout: 15000,\n   120→  navigationTimeout: 30000,\n   121→  reporter: [\n   122→    replayReporter({\n   123→      apiKey: process.env.REPLAY_API_KEY ?? process.env.RECORD_REPLAY_API_KEY,\n   124→      upload: false,\n   125→    }),\n   126→    ['json', { outputFile: 'test-results/results.json' }],\n   127→    ['list'],\n   128→  ],\n   129→  use: {\n   130→    ...replayDevices['Replay Chromium'],\n   131→  },\n   132→});\n   133→```\n   134→\n   135→### 5. Replay CLI commands reference\n   136→\n   137→- `npx replayio list` — List all local recordings (ID, host, date, duration, status).\n   138→- `npx replayio list --json` — Full JSON metadata for all recordings. Each entry includes:\n   139→  `id`, `buildId`, `date`, `duration`, `metadata` (host, URI, sourceMaps, processType),\n   140→  `path`, `recordingStatus` (one of `\"recording\"`, `\"finished\"`, `\"crashed\"`).\n   141→- `npx replayio upload <id>` — Upload a single recording by ID. Returns the viewable URL.\n   142→- `npx replayio upload --all` — Upload all local recordings.\n   143→- `npx replayio remove --all` — Delete all local recordings (use between runs to avoid stale\n   144→  data accumulating).\n   145→\n   146→### 6. OpenSSL 1.1 requirement\n   147→\n   148→The Replay browser's recording driver requires `libcrypto.so.1.1` (OpenSSL 1.1). If the\n   149→container only has OpenSSL 3, the browser will run but silently fail to record (no error in\n   150→test output, just `DoLoadDriverHandle: dlopen failed` in stderr). Fix: download Ubuntu 18.04's\n   151→`libssl1.1` deb, extract it, and set `LD_LIBRARY_PATH` to point at the extracted libs. See\n   152→the deployment Playwright config for a working example.\n   153→\n   154→### 7. Pre-test verification\n   155→\n   156→Before running tests for the first time in an app, verify the Replay browser is installed:\n   157→\n   158→```bash\n   159→ls ~/.replay/runtimes/chrome-linux/chrome\n   160→```\n   161→\n   162→If it does not exist, run `npx replayio install` before any test execution. Running tests\n   163→without the Replay browser means failures produce no recordings, making debugging impossible.\n   164→Do not defer Replay installation to after failures are discovered.\n   165→\n   166→## Pre-Flight Checks\n   167→\n   168→Before running any tests, run the standard pre-flight procedure from `skills/scripts/preflight.md`.\n   169→Run each preflight step as a **separate command** — do NOT chain them with `&&` or `;` in a\n   170→single command. Chained commands fail if any sub-command fails (especially `pkill` when no\n   171→process exists), producing confusing output and wasting effort decomposing and re-running.\n   172→\n   173→```bash\n   174→# Step 1: Kill stale servers (including zombies holding port 8888)\n   175→pkill -f \"netlify|vite\" 2>/dev/null || true\n   176→# If port 8888 is still occupied after pkill (zombie processes invisible to lsof),\n   177→# find PIDs via /proc/net/tcp6 and kill -9 them:\n   178→fuser -k 8888/tcp 2>/dev/null || true\n   179→```"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6af13010-cdc6-4704-9ad1-fa671aac30b2","uuid":"9a6baa67-d779-4556-9c52-e39c592aca50","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/repo/skills/tasks/build/testing.md","content":"`RECORD_REPLAY_API_KEY` must be set in the environment. It is already available in this\ncontainer. The Playwright config passes it to the reporter via:\n\n```ts\napiKey: process.env.REPLAY_API_KEY ?? process.env.RECORD_REPLAY_API_KEY\n```\n\n### 4. Playwright config\n\nThe Playwright config MUST:\n\n1. Import BOTH `replayReporter` and `devices as replayDevices` from `@replayio/playwright`.\n2. Include `replayReporter(...)` in the `reporter` array with `upload: false`. Uploads are\n   handled by the test script, NOT by the reporter.\n3. Spread `replayDevices['Replay Chromium']` into the global `use` config. This sets\n   `executablePath`, `RECORD_ALL_CONTENT`, and critically `RECORD_REPLAY_METADATA_FILE` which\n   is required for the reporter to attach test metadata (pass/fail result) to recordings.\n   Without `RECORD_REPLAY_METADATA_FILE`, the recording driver cannot read the per-test\n   metadata written by the fixture, and all recordings will have empty `testResult`.\n\n**IMPORTANT**: Do NOT manually set `launchOptions.executablePath` and `env` instead of using\n`replayDevices['Replay Chromium']`. Manual config misses `RECORD_REPLAY_METADATA_FILE`,\nwhich breaks the recording-to-test metadata association.\n\n**IMPORTANT**: The Replay Chromium browser adds instrumentation overhead that makes all\noperations ~2–3x slower than standard Chromium. Always set higher timeouts from the start:\n\n- `actionTimeout: 15000` (15s for clicks, fills, etc.)\n- `navigationTimeout: 30000` (30s for page navigations)\n- Use `expect.toBeVisible({ timeout: 10000 })` for post-navigation assertions\n\nExample:\n\n```ts\nimport { defineConfig, devices } from '@playwright/test';\nimport { devices as replayDevices, replayReporter } from '@replayio/playwright';\n\nexport default defineConfig({\n  // Replay Chromium is slower due to instrumentation — use generous timeouts\n  actionTimeout: 15000,\n  navigationTimeout: 30000,\n  reporter: [\n    replayReporter({\n      apiKey: process.env.REPLAY_API_KEY ?? process.env.RECORD_REPLAY_API_KEY,\n      upload: false,\n    }),\n    ['json', { outputFile: 'test-results/results.json' }],\n    ['list'],\n  ],\n  use: {\n    ...replayDevices['Replay Chromium'],\n  },\n});\n```\n\n### 5. Replay CLI commands reference\n\n- `npx replayio list` — List all local recordings (ID, host, date, duration, status).\n- `npx replayio list --json` — Full JSON metadata for all recordings. Each entry includes:\n  `id`, `buildId`, `date`, `duration`, `metadata` (host, URI, sourceMaps, processType),\n  `path`, `recordingStatus` (one of `\"recording\"`, `\"finished\"`, `\"crashed\"`).\n- `npx replayio upload <id>` — Upload a single recording by ID. Returns the viewable URL.\n- `npx replayio upload --all` — Upload all local recordings.\n- `npx replayio remove --all` — Delete all local recordings (use between runs to avoid stale\n  data accumulating).\n\n### 6. OpenSSL 1.1 requirement\n\nThe Replay browser's recording driver requires `libcrypto.so.1.1` (OpenSSL 1.1). If the\ncontainer only has OpenSSL 3, the browser will run but silently fail to record (no error in\ntest output, just `DoLoadDriverHandle: dlopen failed` in stderr). Fix: download Ubuntu 18.04's\n`libssl1.1` deb, extract it, and set `LD_LIBRARY_PATH` to point at the extracted libs. See\nthe deployment Playwright config for a working example.\n\n### 7. Pre-test verification\n\nBefore running tests for the first time in an app, verify the Replay browser is installed:\n\n```bash\nls ~/.replay/runtimes/chrome-linux/chrome\n```\n\nIf it does not exist, run `npx replayio install` before any test execution. Running tests\nwithout the Replay browser means failures produce no recordings, making debugging impossible.\nDo not defer Replay installation to after failures are discovered.\n\n## Pre-Flight Checks\n\nBefore running any tests, run the standard pre-flight procedure from `skills/scripts/preflight.md`.\nRun each preflight step as a **separate command** — do NOT chain them with `&&` or `;` in a\nsingle command. Chained commands fail if any sub-command fails (especially `pkill` when no\nprocess exists), producing confusing output and wasting effort decomposing and re-running.\n\n```bash\n# Step 1: Kill stale servers (including zombies holding port 8888)\npkill -f \"netlify|vite\" 2>/dev/null || true\n# If port 8888 is still occupied after pkill (zombie processes invisible to lsof),\n# find PIDs via /proc/net/tcp6 and kill -9 them:\nfuser -k 8888/tcp 2>/dev/null || true\n```","numLines":100,"startLine":80,"totalLines":944}}}
